     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "173ca4cb-9498-4427-a609-f85aaa098a08"],
     ", \(q\)]\)). The field content of the corresponding operator is (",
     Cell[BoxData[
     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "bdd2b125-5516-469d-862e-09736d8ce712"],
     ", \(1\)], \(_\)]\)",
     Cell[BoxData[
     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "c9ccf3da-96db-48e5-959f-559bd5f959ba"],
     ", \(2\)]\))(",
     Cell[BoxData[
     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "03fec80c-3487-4ddd-a2f6-9670ed500571"],
     ", \(1\)], \(_\)]\)",
     Cell[BoxData[
     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "c1ae2cbb-2076-465e-9852-63434207c8a6"],
     ", \(2\)]\)) with ",
     Cell[BoxData[
     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "6f7c6d80-4b98-4734-9bd0-dc1ef3631d34"],
     ", \(1, 2\)]\)\[Element]{e,\[Nu]}, ",
     Cell[BoxData[
     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "097d65c7-3057-43d0-85aa-be099f5f6588"],
     ", \(1, 2\)]\)\[Element]{u,d} and \[Alpha],\[Beta],i,j\[Element]{1,2,3} \
are the corresponding flavor indices. The type of the form factors are \
divided into regular and singular: {\"regular\",{",
     Cell[BoxData[
     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "da4a29a9-58ab-41c9-8ea3-454af69c06e7"],
     ", \(s\)]\),",
     Cell[BoxData[
     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "37f2b2d2-a932-45c5-8ee0-133ae98bda84"],
     ", \(t\)]\)}} is the form factor for an EFT contact interaction with the \
scaling ",
     Cell[BoxData[
     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "839ac9d0-fa79-4623-a5ac-a2ede9c9df99"],
     ", \(^\)], SubscriptBox[\(n\), \(s\)]]\)",
     Cell[BoxData[
     "$Failed"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
      "6706e61c-077e-4106-be27-4060b54fcaed"],
     ", \(^\)], SubscriptBox[\(n\), \(t\)]]\) in the partonic Mandelstam \
variables, whereas {mediator, 0 | SM} is the form factor for a non-local \
interaction through the specified mediator \[Element] {\"Photon\", \
\"ZBoson\", \"WBoson\", ...}. The singular form factors are divided into \
Standard Model contributions (SM) and NP contributions (0).\nFF \[Rule] bool \
is an option that specifies whether the output should be given in terms of \
form factors (bool=True) or in terms of Wilson coefficients or coupling \
constants (bool=False), where the latter is the default. This option can be \
used with: DifferentialCrossSection, CrossSection, EventYield, ChiSquareLHC."
    }], "TableText",ExpressionUUID->"fb172982-a703-4141-8e22-deb866ba2550"]},
